### What is the problem?

Given a string, find the length of the longest contiguous substring that contains no repeated character. For "abcabcbb", the answer is 3 because "abc" is the longest substring with all unique characters. This is a classic sliding window problem that appears frequently in coding interviews.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class LongestUniqueSubstring {
    public static int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        Set<Character> window = new HashSet<>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            while (window.contains(s.charAt(right))) {
                window.remove(s.charAt(left));
                left++;
            }
            window.add(s.charAt(right));
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

Rajesh at the National Bank of India monitors a stream of operation codes for transaction processing. A long run of distinct codes indicates healthy diversity in operations. A short run means codes are repeating quickly, which could signal a stuck process or a retry loop. The longest unique substring length serves as a quick health metric.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dsa.LongestUniqueSubstring;

public class OperationHealthService {
    public void checkDiversity(String operationCodes) {
        int longest = LongestUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring(operationCodes);
        System.out.println("Longest distinct run: " + longest);
        if (longest < 3) {
            System.out.println("WARNING: Low operation diversity, possible retry loop");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new OperationHealthService().checkDiversity("ABCDEFAB");
        new OperationHealthService().checkDiversity("AABBA");
    }
}
```

```text
Longest distinct run: 6
Longest distinct run: 2
WARNING: Low operation diversity, possible retry loop
```

### How does the sliding window work step by step?

Maintain a window defined by `left` and `right` pointers and a HashSet of characters inside the window. Move `right` forward one character at a time. Before adding the new character, check if it already exists in the set. If it does, remove characters from the left end until the duplicate is gone. Then add the new character and update the best length.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class StepByStepDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCBA";
        Set<Character> window = new HashSet<>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            while (window.contains(s.charAt(right))) {
                System.out.println("  Remove s[" + left + "]='" + s.charAt(left) + "'");
                window.remove(s.charAt(left++));
            }
            window.add(s.charAt(right));
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
            System.out.println("right=" + right + " char='" + s.charAt(right) + "' window=" + window + " best=" + best);
        }
        System.out.println("Answer: " + best);
    }
}
```

```text
right=0 char='A' window=[A] best=1
right=1 char='B' window=[A, B] best=2
right=2 char='C' window=[A, B, C] best=3
  Remove s[0]='A'
right=3 char='B' window=[C, B] best=3
  Remove s[1]='B'
  Remove s[2]='C'
right=4 char='A' window=[A] best=3
Answer: 3
```

### How does the HashMap optimization work?

Replace the HashSet with a HashMap from character to its last-seen index. When a duplicate is found and its stored index is at or after the current left pointer, jump `left` directly to one past that index instead of removing characters one by one. This avoids the inner while loop and does fewer operations.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class OptimizedUniqueSubstring {
    public static int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> lastSeen = new HashMap<>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (lastSeen.containsKey(c) && lastSeen.get(c) >= left) {
                left = lastSeen.get(c) + 1;
            }
            lastSeen.put(c, right);
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(lengthOfLongestSubstring("ABCBDA")); // 4 (CBDA)
    }
}
```

```text
4
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Both approaches run in O(n) time. In the HashSet version, the left pointer only moves forward across the whole string and the right pointer also only moves forward, so each character is added once and removed at most once. In the HashMap version, there is no inner loop at all. Space is O(min(n, alphabet size)) for the set or map.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ComplexityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Worst case for HashSet version: "abcd...z" then repeat
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c = 'A'; c <= 'Z'; c++) sb.append(c);
        sb.append(sb); // "ABCD...ZABCD...Z"
        System.out.println("Length: " + sb.length());
        System.out.println("Longest unique: " + LongestUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring(sb.toString()));
    }
}
```

```text
Length: 52
Longest unique: 26
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty string returns 0. A single character returns 1. A string of all identical characters returns 1 because every window of size 2 or more has a duplicate. A string of all unique characters returns its length.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring(""));      // 0
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring("A"));     // 1
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring("AAAA"));  // 1
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring("ABCD"));  // 4
    }
}
```

```text
0
1
1
4
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most critical mistake with the HashMap variant is forgetting the `>= left` check. Without it, a stale index from before the current window incorrectly pulls the left pointer backward, shrinking or corrupting the window. The second mistake is updating best before ensuring the window is valid.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    // WRONG: no >= left guard
    public static int buggyVersion(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> last = new HashMap<>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (last.containsKey(c)) {
                left = last.get(c) + 1; // BUG: may pull left backward
            }
            last.put(c, right);
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // "ABBA" exposes the bug
        System.out.println("Buggy:   " + buggyVersion("ABBA"));   // may give wrong answer
        System.out.println("Correct: " + OptimizedUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring("ABBA")); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy:   3
Correct: 2
```

### How do you use an array instead of a HashMap?

For a fixed small alphabet like ASCII, replace the HashMap with an int array of size 128 initialized to -1. Use the character's ASCII value as the index. This avoids hashing overhead and is faster for character-level operations.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ArrayOptimizedSubstring {
    public static int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        int[] lastSeen = new int[128];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(lastSeen, -1);
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (lastSeen[c] >= left) {
                left = lastSeen[c] + 1;
            }
            lastSeen[c] = right;
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(lengthOfLongestSubstring("HDFC2001ABCD")); // 9
    }
}
```

```text
9
```

